OK, that should do it.

Bob the Barbarian *finally* ascended.  I actually won the game once before
but it was by using a saved file so it didn't count.  I won't bother
listing stats, A.C., intrinsics, and stuff.   Anyway, here are some
highlights of the game (in no particular order) as well as some random
comments...

First of all, the game crashed while I was on the castle level.  I seem to
remember reading something about a bug with things stuck in walls that
are kicked free.  (I'm running 3.1.1 on a Mac.)  Well, I killed a xorn
and kicked out the wand that was stuck in the wall.  A few minutes later,
the game quit by itself.  Luckily it automagically saved.  I tried to run
the game again, but it started at level 1.  Hmmm.  I checked the save file
and noticed that it was called Bob-save/1-e (or something like that).  I
got rid of the "-e" and tried it again and it restored.  Whew!  But wait! 
When I got into the castle all of the objects inside were gone, even the
wand of wishing!  Arrrgghhhh!  Yes, Virginia, you can win without that
particular WoW.

I *did* get a wish from a magic lamp earlier in the game so I had gray
dragon scale mail.  But, being a total bonehead, I tried to enchant it
beyond the limit, and so it was hosed.  I had found some +5 elvish mithril
armor so it got me by until I killed an orange dragon later and got some
orange dragon scale mail, but it's just not the same as GDSM...  I also got
a wish from a sitting on a throne and got the Platinum Yendorian Express
Card. This has got to be one of the most useful items in the game.  At the
time, I didn't realize it also gave you magic protection in addition to ESP
and charging.  What else does this thing do? (I'm not sure that you should
be able to wish for quest items, but since it is currently allowed, I'll
continue to do it :-)

After cleaning out the gnomish mines and offering out the wazoo, I had only
received Cleaver and it didn't look like I was going to get a decent weapon
any time soon, so I headed back upstairs, and back down into the DoD. 
After a few levels, I happened upon a bones level with (huh? it's still
blessed!?) Vorpal Blade!  I also had grillions of identified gems.  I only
recently found out that you can test for glass gems by putting them inside
chests and kicking them until they shatter (or not).  I had always thought
that those muffled shatters were potions.  Maybe potions shatter as well. 
Anyway, pseudo-identifying glass gems makes identifying real gems  a lot
easier because once you have identified a red gem as glass, all other red
gems are real.  I don't usually worry about trying to make it out of the
dungeon with gems since I play on a Mac and don't care about the high score
(actually, I just try to make it out alive) so I usually spend time tossing
gems to an aligned unicorn for luck.  Which reminds me, is there a limit to
the amount of luck you can get or am I just wasting my time after a certain
number of gems?  (Assuming luck doesn't time out for you, of course.)

The barbarian quest level is pretty easy, I think.  One of the things I do
now is if I find a potion of gain level, rather than drink it immediately
(like I use to do), I wait until I am at level 13 then drink it to get to
14 so I can go on the quest.  Otherwise, you will have to kill a *lot* of
grid bugs to get that extra level.  I think that the best thing to have
before going on this particular quest is a blessed tinning kit - makes
trolls a lot easier to deal with.  Of course you could just genocide them. 
BTW, what happens if you genocide T's and O's?  Are the levels deserted?  I
don't think I will ever do this, because they are a great source for
scrolls and potions.

I found Ft. Knox/Ludios/Whatever in this game.  This was one point where I
was really worried about dying.  One of the soldiers wielded a cockatrice
corpse and headed my way.  Run away!  Run away!  Looks like anyone wearing
gloves can pick up one of those things.  I locked myself up inside the door
and waited long enough to where I thought it would have rotted away.  Maybe
I should have put on my ring of conflict and let him take care of the other
guys :-)  Oh, well.  I kept hearing explosions in the distance so I put on
my blindfold and saw a stalker walking around in the vault.  He got rid of
half of the land mines before it finally killed him.  One of the easiest
ways of going through there is levitating and floating around until the air
currents set them off.  I don't bother messing with ascending with the $'s,
but prefer to donate them to an aligned priest.  I think I could have
walked around butt naked and still have -15 A.C., but I'll keep that armor
on just in case :-)

As for soldiers wielding cockatrices, I had another one try later in the
game, but luckily his spear was welded to his hand.  Ha!

Gehennon, etc. was mostly uneventful.  In another game, my bag of holding
got cursed and got so heavy that I couldn't move, so this time I had *lots*
of holy water with me.  I had Demonbane, which I think was helpful,
although I got blasted sometimes when I wielded it.  The Wizard drank a
cursed potion of gain level and escaped before I got the Book of the Dead,
so I had to work my way back out and then back up to look for him.  What a
hassle.  He appeared quite often later in the game but I had a few wands of
death to take care of him.  I have found that using a lamp makes the mazes
go a lot faster, since you don't have to go down every single dead-end. 
This seems obvious, but I never thought of it before.  Also, having the
PYEC, you can keep recharging it.  By the way, I tried praying inside of
Vlad's tower.  No problem.  Minotaurs seem to be a lot tougher now than in
3.0.  Rather than deal with them, I just genocided H's.  I had also
genocided L's, V's, D's, and n's.  Priests tend to summon a's, so I
genocided them as well to see what would happen in Moloch's level and the
astral plane.  They summoned snakes instead, so next time I will genocide
S's, too.  (I had quite a few blessed genocide scrolls in this game.)

I thought I was going die (although I had several amulets of life saving)
in the plane of fire.  Archons are such a pain in the ass.  They keep
blinding/stunning you with their brilliance, wands of death don't work
against them since they carry shields of reflection, and just about
everyone of them seems to be carrying a wand of create monster.  I finally
got a cockatrice corpse, and that took care of them.  Of course, I was also
surrounded by xorns, which don't stone.  Next time, I think I will genocide
them, and maybe golems as well.  Also, I had always thought that you needed
an amulet of magical breathing on the plane of water in case you wandered
outside of your air bubble, but found out later that you don't.

One somewhat funny message was when I killed something while blinded. 
("You kill it!  But wait... Maybe not...")  Well, I've rambled on long
enough.  Now it's time to try to ascend with Wally the Wonder Wizard.

-cb

P.S.  This was probably the looooongest game I have ever played.  So I'm
curious. What is the average length in total time for games in which you
have ascended?

